/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 87 0 R
/T (Traditional malicious URL detection systems primarily rely on blacklist-based approaches, where previously identified malicious websites are stored in a database. Although blacklist methods are effective against known threats, they suffer from several limitations. Newly created phishing websites and zero-day malicious URLs often remain undetected until they are manually reported and added to the blacklist. This delay provides attackers with sufficient time to target a large number of users. Furthermore, maintaining and updating blacklists requires continuous monitoring and manual intervention, making them less effective against rapidly changing cyber threats.)
/E (Traditional malicious URL detection systems primarily rely on blacklist-based approaches, where previously identified malicious websites are stored in a database. Although blacklist methods are effective against known threats, they suffer from several limitations. Newly created phishing websites and zero-day malicious URLs often remain undetected until they are manually reported and added to the blacklist. This delay provides attackers with sufficient time to target a large number of users. Furthermore, maintaining and updating blacklists requires continuous monitoring and manual intervention, making them less effective against rapidly changing cyber threats.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
153 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 87 0 R
/T (To overcome these challenges, Artificial Intelligence \(AI\) and Machine Learning \(ML\) have emerged as powerful solutions for malicious URL detection. Unlike traditional methods, AI-based systems learn patterns and characteristics from large datasets of both benign and malicious URLs. By analyzing various features such as URL length, domain structure, special characters, subdomains, and lexical patterns, machine learning models can accurately predict whether a URL is legitimate or malicious, even if it has never been encountered before.)
/E (To overcome these challenges, Artificial Intelligence \(AI\) and Machine Learning \(ML\) have emerged as powerful solutions for malicious URL detection. Unlike traditional methods, AI-based systems learn patterns and characteristics from large datasets of both benign and malicious URLs. By analyzing various features such as URL length, domain structure, special characters, subdomains, and lexical patterns, machine learning models can accurately predict whether a URL is legitimate or malicious, even if it has never been encountered before.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
