1071 0 obj
<<
/Title (The proposed system utilizes Artificial Intelligence \(AI\) and Machine Learning \(ML\) techniques to analyze various characteristics of a URL, such as its structure, length, domain-related features, and other relevant attributes. Based on these features, the trained model classifies the URL as either benign \(safe\) or malicious, enabling users to avoid potentially dangerous websites.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 943 0 R
/Prev 1025 0 R
/Next 1072 0 R
>>
endobj
1072 0 obj
<<
/Title (Another important objective of this project is to improve the accuracy of malicious URL detection while minimizing false positives and false negatives. Unlike traditional blacklist-based approaches, which can only identify previously known malicious websites, the proposed AI model is capable of recognizing newly generated and previously unseen malicious URLs by learning patterns from historical data.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 943 0 R
/Prev 1071 0 R
/Next 1026 0 R
>>
endobj
1073 0 obj
<<
/Title (The rapid growth of the internet has transformed the way people communicate, conduct business, access information, and perform financial transactions. With the increasing dependence on online platforms for banking, shopping, education, and social networking, the number of internet users has grown significantly over the past decade. While this digital transformation has brought numerous benefits, it has also created new opportunities for cybercriminals to exploit users through various forms of cyberattacks.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1027 0 R
/Next 1081 0 R
>>
endobj
1074 0 obj
<<
/Title (In addition to phishing websites, cybercriminals also distribute malware through malicious URLs. When users unknowingly visit these websites, malware can be automatically downloaded onto their devices, leading to data theft, ransomware attacks, unauthorized access, and financial losses. As cyber threats continue to evolve, malicious websites are becoming more sophisticated and are capable of bypassing conventional security mechanisms.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1027 0 R
/Prev 1081 0 R
>>
endobj
1075 0 obj
<<
/Title (Traditional malicious URL detection systems primarily rely on blacklist-based approaches, where previously identified malicious websites are stored in a database. Although blacklist methods are effective against known threats, they suffer from several limitations. Newly created phishing websites and zero-day malicious URLs often remain undetected until they are manually reported and added to the blacklist. This delay provides attackers with sufficient time to target a large number of users. Furthermore, maintaining and updating blacklists requires continuous monitoring and manual intervention, making them less effective against rapidly changing cyber threats.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1027 0 R
/Next 1082 0 R
>>
endobj
